      "text": "To determine the number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) of real numbers such that \\(10, a, b, ab\\) is an arithmetic progression, we start by recalling the property of an arithmetic progression (AP). For any terms \\(x_1, x_2, x_3, \\ldots, x_n\\) of an AP, the difference between consecutive terms is constant. This constant difference is the common difference, denoted by \\(d\\).\n\nIn the given arithmetic progression \\(10, a, b, ab\\), the three terms are \\(10, a, b\\), and the fourth term is \\(ab\\). The common difference \\(d\\) between the terms \\(a\\) and \\(10\\) is:\n\\[\na - 10 = d\n\\]\n\nThe common difference \\(d\\) between the terms \\(b\\) and \\(a\\) is:\n\\[\na - b = d\n\\]\n\nThe common difference \\(d\\) between the terms \\(ab\\) and \\(b\\) is:\n\\[\nb - ab = d\n\\]\n\nSince all these differences are equal, we can set up the following equations:\n\\[\na - 10 = b - ab = ab - b = d\n\\]\n\nTo find \\(d\\), we use the expression \\(a - 10 = b - ab = ab - b\\). Since \\(a - 10\\) and \\(ab - b\\) are both equal to \\(d\\), we can express \\(ab - b\\) in terms of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\\[\nb - ab = d\n\\]\n\\[\na - 10 = d\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\nb - ab = a - 10\n\\]\n\nNext, we isolate \\(b\\) in the equation:\n\\[\nb - ab = a - 10\n\\]\n\\[\nb(1 - a) = a - 10\n\\]\n\\[\nb = \\frac{a - 10}{1 - a}\n\\]\n\nWe also know that \\(a - 10 = d\\). Substituting \\(d\\) back into the equation for \\(b\\):\n\\[\nb = \\frac{a - d}{1 - a}\n\\]\n\nWe have two expressions for \\(b\\):\n1. \\(b = \\frac{a - 10}{1 - a}\\)\n2. \\(b = \\frac{a - d}{1 - a}\\)\n\nEquating these two expressions for \\(b\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{a - 10}{1 - a} = \\frac{a - d}{1 - a}\n\\]\n\nSince the denominators are the same, we can equate the numerators:\n\\[\na - 10 = a - d\n\\]\n\nSubtract \\(a\\) from both sides:\n\\[\n-10 = -d\n\\]\n\\[\nd = 10\n\\]\n\nNow, substituting \\(d = 10\\) back into the expression for \\(b\\):\n\\[\nb = \\frac{a - 10}{1 - a}\n\\]\n\\[\na - 10 = 10 - b\n\\]\n\\[\na - 10 = -10 + b\n\\]\n\\[\nb = a - 10\n\\]\n\nThus, the pairs \\((a, b)\\) are given by:\n\\[\na - 10 = b - a\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\\[\nb = \\frac{a + 10}{2}\n\\]\n\nWe now need to ensure that the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are valid. For \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) to be real numbers, the expression \\(\\frac{a + 10}{2}\\) must be a real number, which is always true for real \\(a\\). Therefore, \\(a\\) can be any real number.\n\nTo find the number of possible pairs \\((a, b)\\), we note that for each real number \\(a\\), there is a corresponding real number \\(b = \\frac{a + 10}{2}\\). Hence, there are infinitely many pairs \\((a, b)\\).\n\nThus, the number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) of real numbers such that \\(10, a, b, ab\\) is an arithmetic progression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\infty}\n\\]",
