      "text": "To find the number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) of real numbers such that \\(10, a, b, ab\\) forms an arithmetic progression, we start by defining the common difference \\(d\\) of this arithmetic progression. The terms of the arithmetic progression are \\(10, 10+d, 10+2d, 10+3d\\). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\nab = 10 + d\n\\]\n\\[\nb = 10 + 2d\n\\]\n\\[\nab = 10 + 3d\n\\]\n\nWe can equate the second and third expressions for \\(ab\\):\n\n\\[\n10 + 2d = 10 + 3d\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\(10\\) from both sides gives:\n\n\\[\n2d = 3d\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\(2d\\) from both sides gives:\n\n\\[\n0 = d\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(d = 0\\). This means the arithmetic progression is simply \\(10, 10, 10, 10\\). Substituting \\(d = 0\\) into the expressions for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) gives:\n\n\\[\n10 = 10 + 0 \\Rightarrow a = 10\n\\]\n\\[\nb = 10 + 2 \\cdot 0 = 10\n\\]\n\\[\nab = 10 \\cdot 10 = 100\n\\]\n\nSince we have found \\(a = 10\\) and \\(b = 10\\), which is the same point in the plane, there is exactly one pair \\((a, b)\\) that satisfies the given conditions.\n\nThus, the number of pairs \\((a, b)\\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
